WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.437
Security requirements define expectations before design and implementation begin.

2
00:00:05.437 --> 00:00:17.279
They connect business goals, user roles, data sensitivity, privacy expectations, availability needs, logging requirements, and regulatory or contractual obligations at a high level.

3
00:00:17.279 --> 00:00:29.269
Without these expectations, teams are forced to make security decisions later from incomplete context, and different engineers may choose inconsistent patterns for the same product risk.

4
00:00:29.269 --> 00:00:34.632
Good requirements describe what must be protected and how success will be evaluated.

5
00:00:34.632 --> 00:00:41.335
A product that handles sensitive customer records needs different controls than a public marketing site.

6
00:00:41.335 --> 00:00:48.112
A feature used by administrators needs stronger access controls than a feature used by anonymous visitors.

7
00:00:48.112 --> 00:00:57.198
Security acceptance criteria help product owners, developers, testers, and operations teams agree on when a feature is safe enough to release.

8
00:00:57.198 --> 00:01:01.965
Abuse cases are useful because they describe how a feature might be misused.

9
00:01:01.965 --> 00:01:03.901
They do not need to be dramatic.

10
00:01:03.901 --> 00:01:08.593
A password reset flow can be abused if it reveals whether an account exists.

11
00:01:08.593 --> 00:01:15.445
A file upload feature can be abused if it accepts unexpected file types or stores content in unsafe locations.

12
00:01:15.445 --> 00:01:20.659
A reporting API can be abused if it allows users to retrieve data outside their role.

13
00:01:20.659 --> 00:01:23.265
Risk tolerance also belongs in planning.

14
00:01:23.265 --> 00:01:29.000
Not every issue has the same urgency, and not every control is appropriate for every system.

15
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:36.001
The important practice is to document assumptions, decision makers, acceptance criteria, and review points.

16
00:01:36.001 --> 00:01:44.938
Clear requirements give engineers a security target they can build toward, and they give testers and operators something concrete to verify.
